  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1205 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nWell we are really doubling down on the cooler air today, as low\nclouds persist across most of the region. There is a small break\nover eastern portions of southeast Missouri where temperatures\nmay climb closer to originally forecast high temperatures, but\nelsewhere, highs are only expected to reach the upper 60s to\nlower 70s this afternoon. Although guidance has no clue at this\npoint, it is likely that the low clouds will linger into, if not\nthrough Wednesday. Therefore, the NBM highs on Tuesday may be a\nbit on the warm side again.\n\nThe upper low over northwest Illinois will drift east southeast\nand should eventually reach the Ohio Valley by the end of the\nday Wednesday. Once this happens, clouds should become less\nprevalent. An upper-level ridge will build over the Quad State\nThursday into Friday and that will lead to some beautiful\nweather to end the work week. \n\nThe latest guidance continues to show some potential for energy\nto quickly suppress the ridge allowing for some chance of \nshowers or thunderstorms over the weekend or into next week. \nUnfortunately, the overall trend is for this to occur later and\nconfidence in timing is very low. Besides, most upper \nhighs/ridges have tended to linger over our area this \nsummer/fall, so there is a possibility that it could hold firm \nthrough the weekend and into next week. The NBM has some 20-30% \nPoPs overspreading the entire area next Tuesday with virtually \ndry conditions until then. At some point when the ridge does try\nto break down, south winds and a warming trend will develop, \nbut the timing of that is also difficult to call at this time. \nIn the meantime, enjoy this fall-like weather!",
